
An official, fully cited zoological species name consists of four primary components: the generic name, the specific epithet, the taxonomic authority, and the date of publication.
While a species name is commonly written as a simple two-word "binomial," its formal designation under the International Code of Zoological Nomenclature (ICZN) requires additional metadata to ensure absolute clarity and historical tracking.

The 4 Elements of a Full Zoological Name


Element	Description	Formatting Rules	Example: Balaena mysticetus Linnaeus, 1758
1. Generic Name	Indicates the genus to which the animal belongs.	Capitalized and italicized (or underlined if handwritten).	Balaena
2. Specific Epithet	Distinguishes the specific species within that genus.	Lowercase and italicized.	mysticetus
3. Author (Authority)	The surname of the scientist who first formally described the species.	Regular text (not italicized). Written in full for animals.	Linnaeus
4. Year of Publication	The year the original scientific description was validly published.	Separated from the author by a comma.	1758


The Rule of Parentheses (Taxonomic Changes)

The placement of parentheses around the author and date indicates whether the species remains in its original genus or has been moved:
• Without Parentheses: Balaena mysticetus Linnaeus, 1758
	• Meaning: This species is still classified under the genus Balaena, exactly as Carl Linnaeus originally designated it in 1758.
• With Parentheses: Homo sapiens (Linnaeus, 1758)
	• Meaning: Linnaeus originally described the species in 1758, but he placed it under a different genus at the time (in this case, he originally classified humans as Simia satyrus or alongside other groups before modern reorganization). The parentheses acknowledge his original description while indicating a subsequent taxonomic move.

Optional Subspecies Element (Trinomen)

If a zoological name refers to a specific subspecies, a third italicized word is appended directly after the specific epithet (creating a trinominal name).
• Example: Canis lupus familiaris (the domestic dog)
