The AOU also shows the history of the scientific names. Is there a standard for this?

Yes, there is a global standard for documenting and formatting the chronological history of scientific names, known broadly as a synonymy list (or taxonomic history).
While organizations like the American Ornithological Society (AOS)—historically the AOU—tailor their layout to fit publication constraints, they follow universal conventions governed by the International Code of Zoological Nomenclature (ICZN) and community consensus.
The standard splits into two distinct types of lists: true synonymies (actual name changes) and chresonymies (a record of how names were historically used or misused).

1. Chronological Layout (The Timeline)

A standard synonymy list is strictly formatted chronologically by date of publication, starting with the original description and tracing every subsequent reclassification, misspelling, or invalid description.
text
Genomys albus Smith, 1920: 14 (Original description)
Genomys albus: Jones, 1935: 45 (Subsequent citation/usage)
Albomys albus: Miller, 1950: 202 (New generic combination)
Albomys nitidus Johnson, 1965: 12 (Junior synonym applied to this species)
Use code with caution.

2. The Punctuation Standard (Crucial Distinctions)

The most important part of the standard is the strict use of punctuation to separate true authors of a name from authors who just used the name.
• No Colons (True Authority): If a name is written as Albomys albus Smith, 1920, it means Smith is the person who created and described that species.
• With a Colon (Subsequent Usage): If it is written as Albomys albus: Jones, 1935, the colon signals that Jones did not name the species; he was just a researcher who published a paper about it in 1935 using that name. (Sometimes a period, semicolon, or the word "of" is used instead of a colon, depending on the journal's style guide).

3. Standards for Types of "Synonyms"

When the AOS tracks a name's history, it categorizes how names are related using standard Latin terms or symbols:

Concept	What It Means	Standard Notation
Objective Synonym	Two names based on the exact same physical type specimen (e.g., an author renames a species because the first name was illegal).	syn. obj.
Subjective Synonym	Two names based on different specimens, but scientists later decide they belong to the same species.	syn. subj.
Basionym	The original name a species was given before being moved to a new genus (primarily a botanical term, but used conceptually in zoology).	Original combination
Misidentifications	When a historical author completely misidentified an animal and called it by the wrong name.	Written as [Name] auct. non [Original Author] (meaning "of authors, not of the original creator")


Why This Standard Matters

Without these exact punctuation and chronological standards, biodiversity databases and checklists could not track historical data accurately. A system like the AOS Check-list uses this standard to allow researchers to look at a bird's name in an 1890 paper and immediately trace it forward to the official name used today.
