The AOS Check-list of North American Birds compiles comprehensive history logs for complex species.
The Fork-tailed Flycatcher (Tyrannus savana) provides a classic example. By tracking its historical entry across the AOU/AOS archives, you can see exactly how the punctuation rules dictate the timeline.

The Historical Log Entry

In a standard taxonomic history for this bird, the chronology reads like this:
1.766 Muscicapa Tyrannus Linnaeus, Syst. Nat., ed. 12, 1: 325.
1.825 Milvulus tyrannus: Swainson, Zool. Journ., 2: 123.
1.886 Milvulus tyrannus (Linnaeus) — AOU Check-list, ed. 1: 247.
1.910 Muscivora tyrannus (Linnaeus) — AOU Check-list, ed. 3: 204.
1.983 Tyrannus savana Vieillot, Hist. Nat. Ois. Amer. Sept. 1: 72.
1.985 Tyrannus savana Vieillot — AOU Check-list, ed. 6: 472.

Line-by-Line Punctuation Breakdown


Line 1: The Basionym (Original Description)

1.766 Muscicapa Tyrannus Linnaeus, Syst. Nat., ed. 12, 1: 325.
• The Punctuation: Linnaeus has no parentheses around it, and there is no colon after the species name.
• What it means: Carl Linnaeus is the definitive biological author who first named and described this bird in his 1766 book Systema Naturae. He originally assigned it to the genus Muscicapa.

Line 2: The Subsequent Usage (Chresonymy)

1.825 Milvulus tyrannus: Swainson, Zool. Journ., 2: 123.
• The Punctuation: Note the colon (:) between tyrannus and Swainson.
• What it means: The colon explicitly dictates that Swainson did not invent this species. Instead, he moved it into the genus Milvulus in his 1825 paper. The colon stops researchers from accidentally crediting Swainson as the original discoverer.

Line 3: The First AOU Baseline

1.886 Milvulus tyrannus (Linnaeus) — AOU Check-list, ed. 1: 247.
• The Punctuation: Linnaeus is now wrapped in parentheses.
• What it means: When the AOU published its very first checklist in 1886, they accepted Swainson’s generic change (Milvulus). The parentheses tell you: "Linnaeus named this bird, but he did not call it Milvulus—someone else moved it here later."

Line 4: Another Move

1.910 Muscivora tyrannus (Linnaeus) — AOU Check-list, ed. 3: 204.
• The Punctuation: Parentheses remain around Linnaeus, but the genus changes to Muscivora.
• What it means: By 1910, ornithologists decided the bird belonged to the genus Muscivora. Linnaeus gets original credit, but the parentheses keep reminding you that Muscivora was not his original idea.

Lines 5 & 6: The Modern Shift (Correcting History)

1.983 Tyrannus savana Vieillot, Hist. Nat. Ois. Amer. Sept. 1: 72.
1.985 Tyrannus savana Vieillot — AOU Check-list, ed. 6: 472.
• The Punctuation: Linnaeus completely vanishes from the authority line, replaced by Vieillot without parentheses.
• What it means: In the 1980s, taxonomists discovered that Linnaeus's original 1766 description was fundamentally flawed or legally invalid under code rules for this specific population. A French ornithologist named Louis Pierre Vieillot had independently published a valid description of the bird in 1808 as Tyrannus savana. Because Vieillot originally placed it in Tyrannus and it is still in Tyrannus, his name features no parentheses. The AOU officially adopted this in 1885.
